<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.10.1: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00542</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.10.1: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b240e009-256b-4d80-9b51-bcf482af45c6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Kiran is getting dinner for his drama club on the evening of their final rehearsal. He
        plans to buy some prepared dishes from a supermarket.</para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>The budget for the dinner is $60.</item>
        <item>The prepared dishes are sold by the pound, at $5.29 per pound.</item>
        <item>He also plans to buy two large bottles of sparkling water at $2.49 each.</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Represent the constraints in the situation mathematically. If you use variables,
            specify what each one means.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer:</para>

          <para id="para-00004">\(5.29p+2(2.49)\leq60\), or \(5.29p+4.98\leq60\). \(p\) is the pounds of prepared
            foods Kiran could buy without going over budget.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">How many pounds of prepared dishes can Kiran buy? Be prepared to show your
            reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>

          <para id="para-00007">Up to 10.4 pounds, or \(p\leq10.4\). For example: After removing the cost of
            sparkling water, Kiran still has $55.02. Dividing that amount by 5.29 gives 10.4.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
  </content>
